  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "What Zhuyin is for",
      "body": "<p>Zhuyin (注音, also called Bopomofo from its first four symbols) is Taiwan's standard way to teach pronunciation beside traditional characters.</p>\n<p>Each syllable is a vertical stack: an optional initial on the left, a medial if needed, a final, then a tone mark on the right.</p>\n<p>Pinyin is useful for typing and for learners from mainland materials, but Zhuyin matches how Taiwanese classrooms mark readers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Initials, medials and finals",
      "body": "<p>聲母 are the consonant starts: ㄅ through ㄙ in this app.</p>\n<p>介音 ㄧ ㄨ ㄩ glide into the vowel; they are not always spelled as separate keys in every syllable chart.</p>\n<p>韻母 carry the main vowel (and sometimes a glide or nasal ending). Put initial + medial + final together, then add the tone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The five tones",
      "body": "<p>First tone is high and level; Zhuyin leaves the block unmarked.</p>\n<p>Second tone ˊ rises; third ˇ dips then rises; fourth ˋ falls sharply.</p>\n<p>Neutral ˙ is short and unstressed, common on particles like 吧 or the second half of 謝謝.</p>\n<p>Mandarin is tonal: changing the tone changes the word.</p>"
    }
  ]
